+# Hunt report: export worker acceptance and restart recovery
+
+Revision: `85d33b3` (Python 3.9.6, SQLite 3.43.2, macOS).
+
+## Result
+
+**No implementation defect found in the bounded coverage below.** Every
+accepted job produced its result after the interruptions checked, and
+completed jobs got no further work. This is a clean bounded hunt. It does
+not show that no bugs exist, and it is not an independent claim verification.
+
+## Scope and stopping condition
+
+- Operation: `Queue.submit` (acceptance), `Queue.work`, and the restart path
+  `Queue.__init__` → `Queue.recover` in `app/export_queue.py`.
+- Contract: `app/export-contract.md`. No `specs/` record governs the export
+  queue. `ARCH-app`, `SPEC-note-payload`, and `CLAIM-single-writer` cover the
+  note `Store`/`data/` path, not the queue's SQLite file. Nothing else in the
+  repo references `export_queue`/`Queue`, so there are no consumers to trace.
+- Harms checked, in priority order: (1) accepted jobs abandoned after an
+  interruption; (2) a false acceptance report, where `submit` says accepted
+  but the job isn't durable; (3) jobs stuck in `running` permanently;
+  (4) completed jobs being redone or their results lost.
+- Stop: after these cases and their recovery path were checked.
+
+## Fault model (from the contract)
+
+- Committed SQLite transactions survive termination. Storage loss and
+  corruption are excluded.
+- There is exactly one worker. A restart begins only after the previous
+  worker has stopped. Requests never overlap.
+- The application retries `work` after transient failures and calls it again
+  once the environment recovers. This is the eventual-recovery assumption.
+- A job ID always has one payload.
+- An exception raised from the `after_claim` hook stands for an interruption
+  after `running` commits and before output is written.
+
+## Code-reading argument
+
+- `submit` commits the `INSERT OR IGNORE` inside `with self.db` before it
+  returns `accepted`, so acceptance always reflects a durable row.
+- `work` commits `running` in its own transaction. The result `INSERT` and
+  the `done` update share a second transaction. That makes "result exists"
+  and "state = done" atomic together, so the plain `INSERT INTO results`
+  (no `OR IGNORE`) can't hit an existing row for a job that isn't done.
+- If the process is interrupted between the two transactions, the row stays
+  in `running`:
+  - On restart, `recover()` runs in the constructor and resets `running` to
+    `pending`.
+  - With no restart (a transient failure), `work` still selects
+    `state IN ('pending','running')`.
+
+  Either way the job is picked up again. Because only one worker exists, a
+  `running` row can't belong to a live peer.
+- A completed job is never selected again. A resubmit with the same ID is a
+  no-op (`INSERT OR IGNORE`), and `recover()` only touches `running` rows.
+
+## Checks run
+
+`python3 tests/hunt_witness.py` exits 0 on this revision (18/18 checks pass).
+It calls the real `Queue` entry points on temporary SQLite files:
+
+| Scenario | Interruption | Expected / observed |
+|---|---|---|
+| kill | child process calls `os._exit` in the `after_claim` hook (after `running` commits) | the row is left `running` on disk; after restart, `work` produces the result and the state is `done` |
+| repeat | three successive killed workers over three jobs | all three results are produced after restart |
+| accept | child process exits right after `submit` returns `accepted` | the job survives and its result is produced after restart |
+| transient | hook raises in-process, then `work` is retried without a restart | the interrupted job and a second job both finish |
+| done | resubmit the same ID/payload after completion, then restart | `work` returns `False` both times and the result is unchanged |
+
+To check that the oracle catches real violations, I ran the witness (via
+`HUNT_APP_ROOT`) against broken copies of `export_queue.py` in a temporary
+directory. Each variant exited 1:
+
+- `recover` disabled and `work` selecting only `pending`: kill, repeat, and
+  transient fail (jobs stuck in `running`).
+- `work` selecting only `pending` (recover intact): transient fails.
+- `submit` without a commit: kill, repeat, and accept fail (acceptance not
+  durable).
+- `recover` marking `running` as `done`: kill and repeat fail (result never
+  produced).
+
+I deleted the scratch mutation script and the copies afterwards. Product
+code, records, and existing evidence are unchanged.
+
+## Gaps and untested conditions
+
+- Interruptions were injected only at the contract's hook boundary and right
+  after `submit`. I did not kill the process inside an SQLite commit. That
+  case depends on SQLite's atomic commit, which the contract assumes.
+- I did not test anything outside the contract: concurrent workers or
+  overlapping requests, a job ID resubmitted with a different payload (the
+  first payload is silently kept), storage loss, or a payload whose export
+  always fails. That last case would block later jobs through
+  `ORDER BY id LIMIT 1`, but the contract's eventual-recovery assumption
+  rules it out.
+- Only one platform and interpreter were tested: Python 3.9.6 and SQLite
+  3.43.2.
